    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000156475679": {
      "title": "Assistant Finance Business Partner",
      "description": "University of Auckland Assistant Finance Business Partner | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Assistant Finance Business Partner Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022449 UoA Department Name: Finance Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an enthusiastic and proactive Assistant Finance Business Partner to join our team on a fixed-term basis through until 31 December 2027. This is an excellent opportunity for an aspiring finance professional looking to broaden their management accounting and business partnering experience within a large, complex organisation. Working closely with Finance Business Partners and key stakeholders, you will provide financial analysis, reporting, budgeting and forecasting support that helps drive sound decision-making and organisational performance. You'll gain exposure to a diverse range of projects, programmes and operational activities while developing your stakeholder engagement and business partnering capability in a supportive and collaborative environment. Key Responsibilities: Business Partnering - Support the Business Partnering team in developing relationships across the Faculty to ensure customer needs and expectations are understood and managed. Operational Activities - Support the provision of consolidated financial services for the Faculty including business intelligence, financial services, and financial administration. Support the FBPs in a range of finance activities. Financial Strategy, Planning and Performance - Support the development of Finance strategies and frameworks that align with the strategic aims of the University, balancing local and organisation-wide requirements. Culture, capability, development and training - Support the FBPs to develop a high-performing function, working collaboratively with finance team members, customers and stakeholders. This is a fulltime, fixed-term position until December 2027. The salary range for this position is between $73,400 to $90,700, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description . Mō tō mātou rōpū  | About the team OneFinance is responsible for the delivery of customer centric financial management, partnering support and advice to facilitate the efficient operation and performance of the University in meeting its strategic objectives, while at the same time ensuring appropriate governance and controls protect the University’s assets, reputation, and financial viability. He kōrero mōu | About you You may currently be working as an Assistant Accountant, Project Analyst or in a similar finance role and are ready to take the next step in your career. You enjoy working with data, solving problems and translating financial information into meaningful insights for stakeholders. You are collaborative, highly organised and customer-focused, with the confidence to build positive working relationships across a diverse organisation. Most importantly, you are motivated to learn, contribute and continuously improve the way finance supports the University's strategic goals. To be successful in this role, you will bring: A tertiary qualification in Accounting, Commerce or a related discipline (or currently working towards one). Sound understanding of management accounting principles and accounting best practice. Strong analytical skills with the ability to interpret and communicate financial information. Excellent written and verbal communication skills and a keen eye for detail. Advanced Microsoft Excel skills; experience with Power BI would be advantageous. Experience working with ERP systems such as PeopleSoft, SAP or Oracle is desirable Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 3 November 2026 to be considered. Please include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, reach out to Nick Godfrey - [email protected] . Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000156459669": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Kaiako",
      "description": "University of Auckland Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55562995 UoA Department Name: Early Childhood Centres Job Description Join Our Supportive Team of Passionate Early Childhood Educators! Are you a New Zealand-trained and certified Kaiako looking for an inspiring and supportive workplace? At Te Ako o te Tui Early Learning Service , located in the heart of the University of Auckland’s Grafton Campus, we offer a vibrant, purpose-built learning environment designed to support the learning, development and wellbeing of tamariki. As one of our larger early learning services, Te Ako o te Tui provides a dynamic environment where tamariki benefit from thoughtfully designed spaces, strong relationships and opportunities to learn, explore and connect. Our exceptional teacher-child ratios support kaiako to be responsive and intentional in their practice, while building meaningful relationships with tamariki and whānau. You’ll join a collaborative and well-resourced service where high-quality teaching, professional growth and a strong team culture are valued, with a range of university staff benefits including above full pay parity. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a passionate and committed ECE qualified and Certificated Kaiako to join our team at Te Ako o te Tui. Our service is a purpose‑built, well‑established early childhood centre, licensed for 90 tamariki aged 3 months to 6 years. The role is primarily based in our over‑2s setting, with flexibility to support across the full age range as required, in line with the needs of our diverse community. This is a permanent, full-time position (40 hours per week) with a rotational shift that changes fortnightly. The salary range for this role is above full pay parity Step 1: $60,701 to Step 11: $101,719 (see our pay structure HERE ). A full description of the role is available in the position description HERE He kōrero mōu | About you You are an enthusiastic and reflective kaiako who brings both heart and intention to your teaching practice. You are passionate about creating meaningful learning experiences and understand the importance of relationships, responsiveness, and intentional teaching. You are confident working within a team, open to professional growth, and committed to ongoing learning. You value diversity and inclusion, and you strive to create an environment where all kaiako, tamariki and whānau feel a strong sense of belonging. You bring a sound understanding of pedagogical practice, including Te Whāriki , and are competent in planning, assessment, and compliance requirements. You may already have some foundation in te reo Māori, or you are motivated to grow this as part of your teaching journey. Most importantly, you are someone who takes pride in your practice, contributes positively to team culture, and is committed to making a meaningful difference in the lives of tamariki. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking We are seeking a passionate, reflective, and intentional kaiako who is committed to providing high-quality learning experiences and contributing meaningfully to a collaborative team culture. You will: Be a New Zealand qualified and certificated teacher who demonstrates genuine care, respect, and advocacy for tamariki Bring experience working across all age groups, with strong confidence in supporting tamariki within a mixed-age environment, fostering holistic learning and development in a culturally diverse setting Demonstrate strong pedagogical knowledge, with the ability to design and implement intentional teaching strategies, and make learning visible through meaningful assessment and planning Show a clear understanding of Te Whāriki and how it informs curriculum design and responsive teaching practice Actively uphold and reflect Te Tiriti o Waitangi in your practice, with a willingness to grow your understanding of tikanga and te reo Māori Build and sustain respectful, trusting relationships with tamariki, whānau, and colleagues, recognising the importance of whakawhanaungatanga Work collaboratively within a team, contributing to shared thinking, internal evaluation, and continuous improvement Be organised and proactive in managing planning, assessment, and compliance responsibilities Demonstrate a growth mindset, engaging in reflective practice, professional learning, and ongoing development. Contribute positively to team culture, upholding professionalism and high standards of practice. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Above Full Pay Parity (see our pay structure HERE ) and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Discounted parking and childcare Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on‑site amenities Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by Wednesday, 4 November 2026 . Your application should include: A brief cover letter outlining your interest in the role and how your skills align Your updated CV Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Indira Boppana, Centre Leader via i.boppana@auckland.ac.nz #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator - Fixed Term Auckland, New Zealand Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Auckland, New Zealand Kaiako - Kōhanga Reo | Early Childhood Education Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000156457949": {
      "title": "Programme Coordinator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Programme Coordinator | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Programme Coordinator Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60022817 UoA Department Name: Academic Services Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an organised Programmes Coordinator to join our Academic Services team. This role provides essential administrative support across a range of student and academic processes, helping to ensure the smooth delivery of academic programmes and an exceptional student experience. Supporting the Faculty of Business and Economics and the Faculty of Law, you’ll be working closely with academic leaders and professional staff supporting coordination of key academic activities, committee operations, contribute to programme administration, and build strong collaborative relationships across the University. This is an excellent opportunity for someone who enjoys variety, takes pride in providing high-quality service, and thrives in a collaborative environment. This position offers the opportunity to broaden your experience in a complex university environment while contributing to the effective delivery of academic services. Key Responsibilities: Provide administrative support for academic programme and student-related processes. Coordinate faculty-based academic activities, including scholarships, prizes, and programme administration. Support academic committees, boards, and meetings, including agenda preparation, minute taking, and meeting coordination. Assist with programme maintenance and course administration activities. Build and maintain effective working relationships with academic and professional staff across the University. Contribute to continuous improvement initiatives and service excellence. This is a fulltime, fixed-term position for 14 months. The salary range for this role is $56,400 - $71,000 depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please review the position description He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for a reliable, collaborative, highly motivated, and focused individual with strong interpersonal skills and a commitment to service excellence. You will manage competing priorities, work independently and collaborate effectively with colleagues across different teams. You will bring: Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple tasks, deadlines and end-to-end processes. Excellent attention to detail, including proofreading and preparing accurate documents. Strong written and verbal communication skills and the confidence to work with a diverse range of stakeholders. The ability to navigate online systems and use digital tools to manage information and administrative processes. Sound problem-solving skills and an interest in identifying opportunities to improve how work is done. The ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team. Experience in providing committee support, working in a large or complex organisation, or supporting academic administration in a tertiary environment would be an advantage. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 1 November 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. Please also advise which position you are applying for. If you have any questions, please email Min-Young Lee via min.lee @auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000156454770": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Kaiako",
      "description": "University of Auckland Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60022865 UoA Department Name: Early Childhood Centres Job Description Join Our Supportive Team of Passionate Early Childhood Educators! Are you a New Zealand-trained and certified Kaiako looking for an inspiring and supportive workplace? At Te Tupu o te Tōrea Early Learning Service , located in the heart of the University of Auckland’s City Campus, our centre is grounded in a strong sense of identity, place, and purpose. Built on a historical nesting ground for the tōrea (oystercatcher), our name reflects growth, protection, and belonging—values that guide our everyday practice. We are proud to offer a “home away from home” for our diverse University community, where relationships are at the heart of everything we do. With exceptional teacher-child ratios, a thoughtfully designed environment, and a commitment to high-quality, intentional teaching, we create a space where both tamariki and kaiako can thrive. Alongside a supportive team culture, we offer a range of staff benefits, including above full pay parity, making this an exciting opportunity to grow your career within a progressive and well-resourced service. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a passionate and committed ECE qualified and Certificated Kaiako to join our team at Te Tupu o te Tōrea. Our centre is licensed for 55 children aged 3 months to 6 years. This role works across our mixed-age setting, with flexibility to support tamariki across the full age range, reflecting the diverse needs of our community. This is a permanent, full-time position (40 hours per week) with a rotational shift that changes fortnightly. The salary range for this role is above full pay parity Step 1 - $60,701 to Step 11 - $101,719 (see our pay structure HERE ). A full description of the role is available in the position description HERE He kōrero mōu | About you You are an enthusiastic and reflective kaiako who brings both heart and intention to your teaching practice. You are passionate about creating meaningful learning experiences and understand the importance of relationships, responsiveness, and intentional teaching. You are confident working within a team, open to professional growth, and committed to ongoing learning. You value diversity and inclusion, and you strive to create an environment where all kaiako, tamariki and whānau feel a strong sense of belonging. You bring a sound understanding of pedagogical practice, including Te Whāriki , and are competent in planning, assessment, and compliance requirements. You may already have some foundation in te reo Māori, or you are motivated to grow this as part of your teaching journey. Most importantly, you are someone who takes pride in your practice, contributes positively to team culture, and is committed to making a meaningful difference in the lives of tamariki. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking We are seeking a passionate, reflective, and intentional kaiako who is committed to providing high-quality learning experiences and contributing meaningfully to a collaborative team culture. You will: Be a New Zealand qualified and certificated teacher who demonstrates genuine care, respect, and advocacy for tamariki Bring experience working across all age groups, with strong confidence in supporting tamariki within a mixed-age environment, fostering holistic learning and development in a culturally diverse setting Demonstrate strong pedagogical knowledge, with the ability to design and implement intentional teaching strategies, and make learning visible through meaningful assessment and planning Show a clear understanding of Te Whāriki and how it informs curriculum design and responsive teaching practice Actively uphold and reflect Te Tiriti o Waitangi in your practice, with a willingness to grow your understanding of tikanga and te reo Māori Build and sustain respectful, trusting relationships with tamariki, whānau, and colleagues, recognising the importance of whakawhanaungatanga Work collaboratively within a team, contributing to shared thinking, internal evaluation, and continuous improvement Be organised and proactive in managing planning, assessment, and compliance responsibilities Demonstrate a growth mindset, engaging in reflective practice, professional learning, and ongoing development Contribute positively to team culture, upholding professionalism and high standards of practice Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Above Full Pay Parity (see our pay structure HERE ) and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Discounted parking and childcare Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on‑site amenities Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by Wednesday, 4 November 2026 . Your application should include: A brief cover letter outlining your interest in the role and how your skills align Your updated CV Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Fathima Subhani, Centre Leader via fathima.subhani@auckland.ac.nz #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator - Fixed Term Auckland, New Zealand Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Auckland, New Zealand Kaiako - Kōhanga Reo | Early Childhood Education Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. × Continue Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
