    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000156195539": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Bioinformatician",
      "description": "University of Auckland Bioinformatician | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Bioinformatician Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022471 UoA Department Name: Operations Administration Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and health researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences is a diverse faculty with more than 4,000 students studying across six schools, led by world-leading researchers.  We are committed to improving the health of our local, national and global communities through excellence in teaching, research, service and engagement. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Precision Medicine Initiative (PMI) is a collaborative programme based at Waipapa Taumata Rau, University of Auckland. It focuses on applying genomics and data-driven approaches to improve diagnosis, treatment, and care. PMI is hosted by the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences (FMHS) in partnership with the Auckland Academic Health Alliance, working at the interface of research and clinical practice. We are seeking a Bioinformatician on a 2-year fixed term to partner with key stakeholders inside and outside the University of Auckland, contributing to the clinical and research genomics work of the Precision Medicine Initiative. You will support the development, implementation, and routine delivery of accredited clinical pipelines, and contribute to research projects through the design and execution of reproducible workflows. The role includes quality assurance and interpretation of sequencing data, often linked to clinical metadata. There may also be opportunities to contribute to variant curation and the preparation of material for clinical multi-disciplinary meetings, including molecular tumour boards. While the focus of this role is research and clinical delivery, there is scope for involvement in research projects aligned with PMI’s translational goals. We are looking for someone with strong core bioinformatics skills and a solid understanding of molecular biology or clinical genomics. The ideal candidate will be as comfortable interpreting the significance of genomic data as they are running the pipeline that produced it. This is a full-time , 37.5 hours per week, 24-month fixed term contract . The salary range for this position is $110,700 - $136,700 per annum, depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You will already have bioinformatic experience in the design and analysis and interpretation of research and clinical projects. Experience and strong knowledge and skill across a broad range of advanced bioinformatic techniques is essential. Your ability to perform complex data manipulation and analysis using Python, R, Stata and/or similar statistical software is a must. Strong communication will be essential in this role, both in your ability to work harmoniously within a team environment and independently, and your ability to translate complex data into meaningful insights. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 9 November 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Wesley Larkan via wesley.larkan@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer in Analytical Chemistry - Te Kura Mātai Matū |The School of Chemical Sciences Auckland, New Zealand Research Operation Coordinator Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Bioinformatician Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000158219387": {
      "title": "Teaching Technician",
      "description": "University of Auckland Teaching Technician - MDLS | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Teaching Technician - MDLS Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022952 UoA Department Name: Technical Services Teaching Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The Opportunity We are seeking a hands-on Teaching Technician to support the safe and effective delivery of practical teaching in the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences (FMHS). Working within our Multi-Disciplinary Laboratories (MDLs) with a particular focus on physiology. You will work closely with academic and technical colleagues to prepare and support teaching sessions, maintain specialised equipment, handle biological materials and animal tissue appropriately, and help ensure laboratory activities run smoothly. The role combines practical laboratory preparation, equipment troubleshooting, student support and day-to-day laboratory operations. Key responsibilities include: Preparing, setting up, supporting and packing down practical physiology teaching sessions, including specialised laboratory procedures and demonstrations. Undertaking specialised dissections, tissue collection and preparation, and relevant drug administration procedures in accordance with approved protocols and safety requirements. Maintaining and troubleshooting scientific equipment, carrying out basic repairs within technical competence and arranging servicing or calibration when required. Demonstrating laboratory equipment, techniques and software and assisting academic staff, tutors and students during practical teaching. Preparing and updating technical set-up notes, risk assessments and laboratory records, and supporting safe handling, storage and disposal of materials. Maintaining teaching spaces, managing equipment and consumable stocks, and supporting the testing of new or modified practical activities. This is a full-time (37.5 hours/week), fixed-term opportunity for 24 months , with a salary range of $63,600 to $75,000 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please view the Position Description . Mō tō mātou rōpū  | About The Team The Multi-Disciplinary Laboratories (MDLs) are a cornerstone of teaching within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, supporting thousands of undergraduate and postgraduate students each year. Our Technical Services team provides essential expertise in laboratory operations, compliance and teaching support, ensuring that academic staff and students have the resources, facilities and guidance needed to succeed. As part of the MDLs, the Physiology teaching laboratories support practical teaching across a range of programmes, including Medical Sciences, Medicine, Pharmacy and Māori Health. Teaching activities include hands-on experiments, physiological measurements, specialised scientific equipment and animal tissue dissection, providing students with opportunities to develop and apply their understanding of human physiology. We value collaboration, innovation and continuous improvement, and work closely with academic partners to deliver high-quality practical learning experiences. He kōrero mōu | About You You will be a practical, organised and adaptable laboratory professional with a good understanding of physiology and scientific laboratory methods. You will be comfortable working both independently and as part of a team, communicating clearly with students and colleagues, and responding constructively to changing teaching requirements. Qualifications A Bachelor of Science (BSc) or equivalent qualification in Biomedical Science, or relevant discipline. Ngā Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills/Experience Three to five years of relevant experience, including experience gained during tertiary study, in Science, Biomedical Science, Biological Science or a related discipline. Knowledge and understanding of physiological processes and laboratory procedures. Practical knowledge of scientific laboratory techniques and the operation, routine maintenance and initial troubleshooting of standard laboratory equipment. Good communication, time-management and organisational skills, with strong attention to detail. Ability to adapt to change and work effectively both independently and collaboratively. Commitment to laboratory health and safety, risk assessment, appropriate animal tissue handling and accurate record keeping. A BSc, Honours or MSc qualification in a relevant discipline, at least two years of teaching laboratory experience, knowledge of tertiary teaching laboratories and experience in animal dissection would be advantageous. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What We Offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How To Apply Applications must be submitted online by 18th November 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how your experience meets the skills and requirements detailed above. Please contact Lynn Bradley via [email protected] if you have any questions about the role. Please note that we are happy to answer questions, but applications cannot be accepted by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000158199640": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Venture and Founder Development Lead",
      "description": "University of Auckland Venture and Founder Development Lead | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Venture and Founder Development Lead Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021452 UoA Department Name: Research and Innovation Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s pre-eminent University, with a turnover of $1.6bn, including research revenue of over $300m generated across eight diverse faculties and two research institutes. With over 9,000 staff and 50,000 students, the University of Auckland is one of New Zealand’s most significant and complex entities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is a unique leadership opportunity to shape the University’s founder and venture development pathway and build the team that will deliver it. Working at the intersection of innovation, entrepreneurship and commercialisation, you’ll lead a busy year-round portfolio that helps students, researchers and staff explore opportunities, validate ideas, develop as founders and progress early-stage ventures towards appropriate next-stage support. You will: Lead the strategic development, delivery and continuous improvement of CIE’s founder and venture development portfolio. Shape a coherent pathway from early idea and exploration through validation, venture development and connection to appropriate next-stage support. Build and lead a new Venture & Founder Development team, creating clear priorities, strong ways of working and consistently high-quality delivery. Lead a diverse portfolio of founder and venture initiatives, ensuring programmes, cohorts, mentors, practitioners and external dependencies are well planned and coordinated. Build trusted relationships with founders, researchers, academics, mentors, investors, industry, commercialisation colleagues and ecosystem partners. Strengthen connections between CIE’s venture initiatives and wider opportunities for research translation, commercialisation, investment and venture support. Use evidence, founder insights and emerging venture practices to inform portfolio decisions and continuously improve quality and founder outcomes. Contribute as a member of CIE’s Leadership Team to the Centre’s strategy, performance, culture and organisational development. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent role.  This is a salary band I role, with a salary range from $122,500pa to $146,000pa depending on skills and experience. The nature of CIE’s programmes and events means the role requires flexibility to work outside normal business hours, including evening work as required and occasional weekend work. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description . Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is Waipapa Taumata Rau’s platform for developing the innovators, entrepreneurs and changemakers of the future. We work across the University to help students, researchers and staff build innovation and entrepreneurial capability, turn ideas into action, develop ventures, and create social, cultural, environmental and economic impact for Aotearoa and the world. CIE is entering an exciting new chapter. With an ambitious new strategy, we are significantly increasing the reach and impact of innovation and entrepreneurship across the University, helping realise Waipapa Taumata Rau’s ambition to be a global powerhouse of innovation, creativity and entrepreneurship, and strengthening the University’s contribution to a more innovative and prosperous Aotearoa. We are building a team of people who are curious, collaborative, ambitious for impact and committed to turning ambition into action. If you want to help shape how a world-class research-led university develops innovative and entrepreneurial people, we would love to hear from you. He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced people and portfolio leader who combines strong venture and founder-development expertise with exceptional organisation, forward planning and execution. You understand what founders and early-stage ventures need as they move from an initial idea through exploration, validation and venture development, and you know how to turn that understanding into high-quality programmes and pathways. You thrive in a fast-paced and varied environment and can lead a complex portfolio while building a new team and establishing the clarity, priorities and ways of working that enable people to perform at their best. You think ahead, anticipate dependencies and pressure points, and remain close enough to delivery to understand what is working, where quality can be strengthened and when intervention is needed. You bring strong commercial judgement and can work credibly across different venture contexts, including student-led and research-derived ventures. You understand research translation and commercialisation and can work effectively with founders, researchers, academics, commercialisation professionals, investors, industry and the wider entrepreneurial ecosystem. You are also a collaborative organisational leader. As a member of CIE’s Leadership Team, you will bring your expertise and perspective to collective decisions, work effectively across portfolio boundaries and take shared responsibility for CIE’s strategy, performance, culture and overall success. You will bring: Significant experience in venture and founder development, entrepreneurship, innovation, commercialisation or a related field. Demonstrated success leading and developing people and building high-performing, collaborative teams. Exceptional forward planning and portfolio-management capability, with experience leading multiple programmes or initiatives in complex, fast-moving environments. Strong understanding of founder journeys and early-stage venture development, including opportunity exploration, validation and progression towards appropriate next-stage support. Strong commercial judgement and an understanding of research translation and commercialisation. Outstanding stakeholder engagement and influencing skills, with the credibility to work effectively with founders, researchers, academics, commercialisation professionals, investors, industry and ecosystem partners. Experience coaching, mentoring or supporting founders as they navigate uncertainty, test assumptions and develop opportunities. Strategic judgement and the ability to translate organisational priorities into coherent pathways, high-quality delivery and measurable outcomes. Experience managing budgets, resources and operational planning. A relevant tertiary qualification in business, innovation, entrepreneurship, science, engineering or a related discipline. Experience in a tertiary education, research, innovation or entrepreneurship environment would be advantageous. An understanding of a research-led university environment, including the different contexts and motivations of students, researchers and academic staff, would be particularly valuable. Additional Information What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary ($122,500 pa to $146,000pa) reflecting experience with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 15th November , 2026 to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter highlighting how you meet the skills and experience outlined above, what attracts you to this opportunity, what you would bring to CIE and its leadership team, and how your experience has prepared you to lead a complex and diverse founder and venture development portfolio within a University context. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend Posted by Joshua Chapman share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Auckland, New Zealand Doctoral Candidate Advisor Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer - Inclusive Education Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Venture and Founder Development Lead Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000158165509": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Procure to Pay Administrator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Procure to Pay Administrator | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Procure to Pay Administrator Contract Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55559763 UoA Department Name: Shared Transaction Centre Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is a great place to work! We have a collaborative and inclusive environment and offer many benefits not offered by other organisations. The Shared Transaction Centre (STC) is the University of Auckland’s centralised ‘Procure to Pay’ and ‘Order to Cash’ centre. It is responsible for all purchasing, billing, and revenue collection activity for faculties and most service divisions.  We perform an operational function, consolidating high volume finance transactional activities relating to the purchasing and supply of goods and services. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for an Accounts Payable Administrator to join the Shared Transaction Centre on a fixed term contract. Support the Accounts Payable Team Leader to work with internal and external stakeholders, maximise automation financial processing, administrative efficiency and improve service delivery across the organisation. Key accountabilities for this position include, but not limited to: Large supplier account reconciliations Requisition requests High volume invoice processing Match exceptions resolutions Receipting escalations Supplier maintenance, onboarding and set-up Co-ordination and execution of foreign and domestic pay runs, working with Treasury, signatories, and the banks. Identify technical roadblocks and opportunities, and suggesting improvements Financial Delegations/Sensitive Expenditure Processing Travel/expenses and reimbursements Accurately matching invoices to Purchase Orders Escalation of issues to the advisory team for internal/external customers Managing the accounts payable function for your portion of the ledger Accurately matching invoices to Purchase Orders Weekly and monthly payment runs Other Ad Hoc duties This is a full-time role (37.5 hours per week, Monday to Friday). Salary range is $62,100 - $70,200pa plus benefits. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You’ve worked in a complex, high‑volume environment, thrive under time pressure, and stay current with accounts payable/P2P best practice. You’re a people person with a can‑do attitude who balances accurate, high‑throughput processing with excellent service to students and corporate customers (transactions, refunds, invoice payments). Ngā Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Excellent organisational and analytical skills. Experience using an ERP (e.g., PeopleSoft) and strong Excel proficiency; quick to learn new, industry‑specific systems. Experience in Accounts Payable and Pay to Purchase within a large complex organisation Proven performance in a customer‑focused environment with strong problem‑solving abilities. A clear commitment to policy, control, and compliance in every transaction. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Career development programmes Discounts on health insurance, childcare and childhood education, as well as free flu vaccinations and eye testing A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Qualifications Additional Information Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Friday 6th November , 2026 to be considered. All candidates must have , or be able to obtain, the legal right to work in New Zealand . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend Posted by Joshua Chapman share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Research Operation Coordinator Auckland, New Zealand Kaiako - Early Childhood Educator Auckland, New Zealand IT Procurement Category Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Procure to Pay Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Contract I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000157995168": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Business Development Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Business Development Manager – Postgraduate Māori and Pacific | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Business Development Manager – Postgraduate Māori and Pacific Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019815 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to creating knowledge, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, we're investing in the future of communications practice. Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Help create pathways to postgraduate study for Māori and Pacific communities across Aotearoa. We’re looking for a Business Development Manager – Postgraduate Māori and Pacific to help create stronger pathways into postgraduate study for Māori and Pacific communities across Aotearoa. This is an exciting opportunity to use your relationship-building, business development and engagement expertise to connect people with postgraduate opportunities that support their aspirations, professional development and future goals. You’ll build and nurture trusted relationships with iwi, Māori and Pacific organisations, professional bodies, community groups and key stakeholders, identifying opportunities to strengthen connections with postgraduate study. You’ll develop and manage a strong engagement and recruitment pipeline, promote our postgraduate programmes in ways that resonate with Māori and Pacific audiences, and turn strong stakeholder relationships into new opportunities and sustainable growth. A key part of the role will be business development and lead generation – identifying new partnerships, creating opportunities and connecting the dots between community and workforce needs and the postgraduate opportunities we offer. You’ll also lead and coordinate outreach, engagement and activation activity, working alongside postgraduate recruitment teams, faculties, Māori and Pacific engagement teams, Marketing and Recruitment, and wider Strategic Engagement functions. Your work will be guided by the strategic flightpaths set out in the University’s Pacific strategy, Ala o le Moana , helping ensure that engagement and partnership activity aligns with the University’s aspirations for Pacific learners and communities. You’ll be joining a team that values Manaakitanga, Whanaungatanga and Kaitiakitanga, and a University committed to equity, diversity and its relationship with tangata whenua and Te Tiriti o Waitangi. What you’ll be doing Build and grow trusted relationships with iwi, Māori and Pacific organisations, employers, community groups, professional bodies, and public sector and workforce development organisations. Identify new business development and lead-generation opportunities that can strengthen Māori and Pacific postgraduate participation. Develop and actively manage a Māori and Pacific postgraduate recruitment pipeline, turning relationships and engagement opportunities into measurable outcomes. Promote postgraduate programmes and pathways in ways that connect with the aspirations of Māori and Pacific communities and workforce capability needs. Lead and coordinate targeted engagement, outreach, recruitment initiatives and partnership opportunities. Work collaboratively across faculties, postgraduate programme teams, Marketing and Recruitment, the PVC Māori Office, PVC Pacific Office and wider Strategic Engagement teams. Contribute to a connected Māori and Pacific community across Marketing and Recruitment and Strategic Engagement. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent opportunity. The full salary range is $98,700 to $133,500 per annum, depending on skills and experience (Band H). We have a structured salary framework with three zones: developing (those developing into the role), fully effective (target), and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $93,201 – $107,500 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description. He kōrero mōu | About you You’ll be a confident relationship builder who knows how to open doors, build trust and create mutually valuable partnerships. You may come from a business development, sales, recruitment, partnership management, stakeholder engagement, community engagement or relationship management background. What matters most is your ability to build authentic relationships and turn those relationships into meaningful outcomes. You’ll bring: Strong relationship-building, networking, influencing and partnership development skills. Experience identifying and developing business opportunities or stakeholder partnerships. Experience working towards targets and managing a pipeline. Strong communication, presentation and proposal-writing skills. Confidence working across different stakeholder groups and organisational environments. Cultural awareness and an inclusive, relationship-centred approach to engagement. Experience working with Māori and/or Pacific communities, organisations or stakeholders. The ability to work autonomously while also collaborating across a complex organisation. Experience in higher education, workforce development, the public sector or another large organisation would be valuable, but we’re interested in the skills, relationships and perspective you can bring. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you’ll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world. Beyond the work itself, you’ll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5-hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns. A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier. Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks’ annual leave and an additional leave buy/sell scheme. Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, and on-site parenting and feeding spaces. A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, and discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff. The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand’s most diverse and complex organisations. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of 15th November 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV, highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities, and relevant experience. Please reach out to Sharon Kishore (Recruitment Consultant) via sharon.kishore@auckland.ac.nz with any questions. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. Reasonable accommodations – We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people – Should you be invited to an interview for this role, you are welcome to have a support person, or whānau member accompany you. Please let us know if there is anything we can do to support you throughout the recruitment process. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Lecturer in Analytical Chemistry - Te Kura Mātai Matū |The School of Chemical Sciences Auckland, New Zealand Marketing Content Creator Auckland, New Zealand Marketing Adviser Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Business Development Manager – Postgraduate Māori and Pacific Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    }
  },
